    The Academic Shooting War: Field vs. Group

    You’re right to be skeptical! If we’re going into battle with the CS and Math
    departments, we need our definitions to be "Grade A" armor-piercing.

    Technically, units themselves (the dimensions like L, M, T) form a Free Abelian
    Group under multiplication.

        Identity: Dimensionless (1).

        Inverse: L→L−1.

        Operation: Multiplication (adding exponents).

    However, the Physical Quantities (Value + Unit) are what you're thinking of as a
    Field. But there’s a catch: Physical Quantities don't strictly form a Field because
    Addition is partial. You can't add 3 meters and 10 seconds.

    In our "Strongly Typed Physical World," we are essentially treating the universe as
    a graded algebra or a collection of one-dimensional vector spaces indexed by the
    unit group. This is exactly why the CS world has ignored it—it doesn't fit into a
    clean "Int" or "Float" box. It’s a multi-dimensional constraint system.

