    The Mutability Paradox

    You've hit on a fundamental tension in software design. Immutability is the
    physicist’s dream: x=5 should always be 5. But Persistence is the engineer’s
    reality: a "Project" evolves.

    If we make Num mutable to support the "Lab Notebook" flow, we lose the ability to
    use Num instances as dictionary keys or in sets—which is a high price to pay for a
    tool intended for complex math.

    The "Tractor" Compromise:

    Keep the Numeric Core (Value, Unit, Type) strictly immutable, but allow the
    Narrative Metadata (the .d attribute) to be mutable.

    In Python, this is a "Cheat" we can pull off by overriding __hash__. If the hash
    only looks at the numeric value and the unit, the object stays "stable" in a set
    even if you change the documentation note.

    def __hash__(self) -> int:
        # The 'd' note doesn't change the physical identity of the number
        return hash((self.real, self.imag, self.numer, self.denom, self.unit))

